Session-2 experiments — adversarial robustness, size curve, interference, rule induction
All numbers produced by python3 run_experiments.py (numpy only, CPU, < 3 min). Raw JSON: results_experiments.json.

P3 — adversarial robustness (200 queries)
The DNA's parser is a rule grammar, so we measured exactly where it breaks.

category	n	parse failures	correct	answered-but-wrong	verdict
control (in-grammar)	32	0	32	0	✅
distractor entity ("I was in Rome last year. What is the capital of France?")	50	0	50	0	✅ robust
multi-clue ("Is the capital of France Rome or Paris?")	14	0	14	0	✅ robust
paraphrase outside the grammar ("France's capital city, please?")	53	53	0	0	❌ parse failure
typo ("What is the capitl of France?")	35	35	0	0	❌ parse failure
negation ("What is NOT the capital of France?")	16	0	0	16	❌ confidently wrong
overall	200	88	96	16	48 %
Honest reading.

Structured attacks (distractor entities, multi-clue) are handled perfectly — the content-addressed lookup is immune to them, which is a real advantage over embedding retrieval.
Lexical attacks (paraphrase outside the grammar, typos) are total failures: the DNA abstains (parse failure → unknown), which is the safe failure mode, but it means the artefact only answers surface forms its grammar covers. This is the single biggest product gap: a real deployment needs a fuzzy/entity-linking front end (or a learned parser) in front of the lattice.
Negation is the dangerous one: the DNA answers "Paris" to "What is NOT the capital of France?" — a confidently wrong answer, not an abstention. The rule algebra has no negation/exception operator. Mitigation path: (a) an explicit "unsupported query type" detector, (b) a negation/exception layer in the rule algebra, (c) never let the DNA answer a question whose surface form contains a quantifier/negation it cannot ground.
P4 — size curve (synthetic geography, realistic name lengths)
concepts	atoms	artifact bytes	bytes/atom	queries	accuracy
100	150	6,349	42.3	100	100 %
500	750	17,583	23.4	500	100 %
1,000	1,502	31,651	21.1	1,002	100 %
31.7 KB for 1,000 concepts — comfortably inside the "≤ 100 KB per 1,000 concepts" success criterion, and ~3× smaller than the 1 MB target.
Bytes/atom falls from 42 → 21 as the fixed overhead (grammar, slot index, namespace header) amortises: the asymptotic cost is ≈ 21 B/atom (≈ 168 bits/atom) for a domain with ~1.5 facts per concept.
Accuracy stays at 100 % across the curve — the slot count scales linearly, no collision cliff.
P5 — interference / corruption
P5b — bit-rot in the packed slot lattice (production mode)
2 % of the bytes of every occupied slot are corrupted (Gaussian, σ proportional to the corruption fraction).

corrupted byte fraction	accuracy	abstention rate	wrong-answer rate
0 %	100 %	0 %	0 %
2 %	30.2 %	69.8 %	0 %
5 %	22.8 %	77.3 %	0 %
10 %	17.5 %	82.5 %	0 %
25 %	16.4 %	83.6 %	0 %
This is the strongest result of the session. The per-slot Reed-Solomon parity converts any corruption into abstention, never a wrong answer. The system loses coverage, not correctness. For a knowledge artifact that is exactly the right trade: "I don't know" is always available, "wrong" never is.

P5a — carrier (wave) lattice under carrier noise
The wave lattice stores each fact's payload repetition-coded over 4 carriers, phase-coded by the entity's deterministic unit-modulus code, and reads it back with a matched filter plus a 2-way interleaved agreement check.

carrier noise σ	accuracy	abstention rate	answered-but-wrong
0	100 %	0 %	0 %
5	2.7 %	68.3 %	~29 %
20	0 %	93.1 %	~6.8 %
60	0 %	97.9 %	~2.1 %
150	0 %	100 %	0 %
Clean operation is exact (100 %), and superposition interference is directly measurable: with cells shared between entities (superpose=True) accuracy at σ=0 drops to 98.4 % instead of 100 % — the cross-talk predicted by the complex-random-code analysis (interference ≈ payload/√D).
But the carrier mode is not safe under noise: ~29 % of the queries it still answers are wrong. The payload is an uncoded 16-bit integer, so a ±2 carrier perturbation flips the id. Fixing this needs a payload ECC (the repetition code gives only a √4 margin).
Conclusion (this is the honest version of the "wave" claim): the carrier/wave encoding buys measurability (SNR, interference, physical absence) and costs ~3× the space (36.1 KB vs 12.8 KB for the same 567 facts), while the packed slot + Reed–Solomon design is the one that is safe (zero wrong answers under corruption). Superposition does not buy density for record-like payloads — it buys interference.
P6 — MDL rule inducer
Given only the atom set (no hand-written rules), the inducer searches for consistent inverse relations and 2-hop chains and scores them by support / description-length:

discovered rule	kind	support	DL	score
capital_of ↔ country_of	inverse	71	2	35.5
country_of ∘ capital_of	chain	71	3	23.7
capital_of ∘ country_of	chain	71	3	23.7
country_of ∘ continent_of	chain	71	3	23.7
The hand-written grammar in world.py encodes exactly: country_of ← capital_of, capital_of_country = country_of . capital_of, continent_of_country = country_of . continent_of.

The inducer rediscovers the hand-written rule set from data alone. This is the mechanism behind D_derive: once rules are learned rather than hand-specified, the derivation factor stops being a hand-tuned constant, and the rule layer can be scaled to domains we never looked at. It also means D_derive can be predicted on a new domain before building anything (see DESIGN.md §9).

