nano_qwen/             dependency-free numpy inference engine for Qwen2 (the teacher)
teacher_bridge.py      probe grid -> teacher answers -> typed-slot extraction -> atoms
helix.py               core: RS codec, slot lattice, rule algebra, encoder, artifact I/O
helix_carrier.py       the *real* wave lattice: phase codes, matched filter, SNR, noise
world.py               domains A (fact-dense) and B (program-generated)
evaluate.py            metrics + coverage-matched baselines
run_experiment.py      main experiment driver
run_teacher_bridge.py  Priority-1 driver: real teacher -> atoms -> DNA-only evaluation
run_experiments.py     P3/P4/P5/P6 experiments
Run it
python3 run_experiment.py        # ~10 s   -> RESULTS.md
python3 run_experiments.py       # ~3 min  -> RESULTS_EXPERIMENTS.md
python3 run_teacher_bridge.py    # ~25 min -> RESULTS_TEACHER.md (needs /tmp/qwen)
Requirements: python ≥ 3.10, numpy. No GPU, no torch, no network at inference time. The teacher engine needs the model once (~1 GB, cached in /tmp/qwen).

What it does
prompt ──► surface parse ──► (entity, relation)
        ──► content address (hash) ──► cell, spiral level
        ──► lattice read ──► wave state z  (payload + SNR + parity check)
        ──► rule algebra (direct | inverse | chain | generative | alias)
        ──► transient map θ_t = A·z (A from a seed ⇒ 0 stored bytes)
        ──► decode answer + confidence + tuple provenance
Measured (this repo, this machine)
Domain A — capitals + 118 elements	Domain B — 4096 generated provinces
artifact	12,770 B	3,387 B
stored atoms	567	32
answerable tuples	449	4,128
queries	869 → 99.9 % correct	12,288 → 100 % correct
unknown probes	12 → 0 false accepts	—
knowledge bits / stored bit	0.105	12.0
vs int8 teacher (0.25 b/param)	0.42× (a loss)	47.9×
