Read the two multipliers differently. Domain A is fact-storage density and it is a loss against a trained int8 teacher — the teacher amortises its interpreter over 10⁸ facts. Domain B is program-compression density (32 atoms + 1 rule cover 4,128 tuples) and it is a win. The lever is D_derive (answerable claims per stored atom), and the rule set can now be induced automatically from atoms alone (P6).

Session-2 additions
Real teacher: nano_qwen/ runs Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct in pure numpy (no torch, no transformers — the sandbox has neither and PyPI is blocked). The bridge extracts atoms from the teacher's free-form answers, then deletes the teacher before evaluation.
Real wave lattice: helix_carrier.py stores facts as phase-coded, repetition-coded complex carriers with a matched-filter read and a measured SNR. It is an interference research substrate, not the production lattice — it costs ~3× the space.
Corruption → abstention, never a wrong error: measured 0 % wrong answers at every corruption level (Reed–Solomon per slot).
MDL rule inducer rediscovers the hand-written inverse + chain rules from atoms.
Limitations (measured, not guessed)
Lexical generality is the product gap. 200 adversarial queries: structured attacks (distractor entities, multi-clue) 100 % correct; out-of-grammar paraphrases and typos 100 % parse failures (safe abstentions, but no answers); negation produces confidently wrong answers (16/16). A learned/fuzzy parser is the next step — not a bigger lattice.
No generative decoding. The decoder returns a retrieved entity string plus a surface template. There is no autoregressive generation and no vocabulary.
Fact domains lose to a trained teacher per byte (0.42×). The win is reliability and absolute size: 12.8 KB answers 869/869 domain probes perfectly where a reliable LLM needs ~1 GB.
The carrier/wave lattice is not corruption-safe without a payload ECC (≈29 % of queries it still answers under noise are wrong). The RS-protected slot lattice is safe.
The teacher is weak. Qwen2.5-0.5B has real factual errors (e.g. Italy → Florence) and weak chemistry recall; those errors propagate into the extracted atoms and set the ceiling for the DNA.
Not measured yet: bit-rot in the carrier lattice with payload ECC, multi-domain artifacts, incremental artifact updates, and a real student decoder.
Baseline used for the teacher comparison
Allen-Zhu & Li, Physics of Language Models 3.3 — Knowledge Capacity Scaling Laws (NeurIPS 2025): language models store 2 bits of knowledge per parameter; int8 preserves that number, int4 drops it to 0.7. Every baseline in RESULTS.md is computed for the same set of answerable tuples as HELIX, so the comparison is coverage-matched.

